"[2602.07160v1] Free Energy Mixer (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07160v1)\nciteturn31571view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07160v1\",\"lineno\":17}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:Free Energy Mixer\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Jiecheng Lu , cite6†Shihao Yang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Free Energy Mixer, by Jiecheng Lu and Shihao Yang\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Standard attention stores keys/values losslessly but reads them via a per-head convex average, blocking channel-wise selection. We propose the Free Energy Mixer (FEM): a free-energy (log-sum-exp) read that applies a value-driven, per-channel log-linear tilt to a fast prior (e.g., from queries/keys in standard attention) over indices.\nL17: Unlike methods that attempt to improve and enrich the $(q,k)$ scoring distribution, FEM treats it as a prior and yields a value-aware posterior read at unchanged complexity, smoothly moving from averaging to per-channel selection as the learnable inverse temperature increases, while still preserving parallelism and the original asymptotic complexity ($O(T^2)$ for softmax; $O(T)$ for linearizable variants).\nL18: We instantiate a two-level gated FEM that is plug-and-play with standard and linear attention, linear RNNs and SSMs. It consistently outperforms strong baselines on NLP, vision, and time-series at matched parameter budgets.\nL19: Comments:  | Camera-ready version. Accepted at ICLR 2026\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2602.07160 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2602.07160v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07160†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | Proceedings of the Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2026)\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jiecheng Lu [cite12†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Fri, 6 Feb 2026 20:02:47 UTC (786 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Free Energy Mixer, by Jiecheng Lu and Shihao Yang\nL35: \nL36:   * cite7†View PDF L37:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite13†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite14†view license L41: \nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L47: \nL48: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2026-02 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite20†cs L53: cite21†cs.AI L54: cite22†cs.LG L55: cite23†stat L56: cite24†stat.ML L57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL66: \nL67: [Button: ×]\nL68: \nL69: loading...\nL70: \nL71: Data provided by:\nL72: \nL73: ### Bookmark\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL76: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Bibliographic Explorer (cite28†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Connected Papers (cite29†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL87: \nL88: Litmaps (cite30†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL89: \n