p space of $\\Conf$.\nL18: The direct limit of finite pure braid groups admits a dense embedding into \\(H^{lf}(\\infty)\\), and we show that \\(H^{lf}(\\infty)\\) is the Ra\\uıkov completion of this subgroup. Moreover, the direct limit of finite braid groups embeds into \\(B^{lf}(\\infty)\\) and is dense in the finitary subgroup \\(B^{lf}_{\\mathrm{fin}}(\\infty)\\subseteq B^{lf}(\\infty)\\).\nL19: Comments:  | 43 pages\nL20: Subjects:  | Algebraic Topology (math.AT); Mathematical Physics (math-ph); General Topology (math.GN); Group Theory (math.GR)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 20F36, 54H11, 57M05\nL22: Cite as:  | cite8†arXiv:2602.07362 [math.AT]\nL23:    | (or cite9†arXiv:2602.07362v1 [math.AT] for this version)\nL24:    | cite10†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07362†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jyh-Haur Teh [cite11†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Sat, 7 Feb 2026 05:08:54 UTC (40 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Topological fundamental groups of locally finite infinite configuration spaces and infinite braids, by Jyh-Haur Teh\nL35: \nL36:   * cite6†View PDF L37:   * cite7†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite12†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite13†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: math.AT\nL44: \nL45: cite14†< prev |   cite15†next > L46: \nL47: cite16†new | cite17†recent | cite18†2026-02 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite19†math L52: cite20†math-ph L53: cite21†math.GN L54: cite22†math.GR L55: cite23†math.MP L56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL66: \nL67: [Button: ×]\nL68: \nL69: loading...\nL70: \nL71: Data provided by:\nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: \nL76: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL87: \nL88: Litmaps (cite29†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL91: \nL92: scite Smart Citations (cite30†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL93: \nL94: [Input] Code, Data, Media\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2602.07375v1] Efficient Post-Training Pruning of Large Language Models with Statistical Correction (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07375v1)\nciteturn31571view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07375v1\",\"lineno\":17}); Total lines: 161\nL9: # Title:Efficient Post-Training Pruning of Large Language Models with Statistical Correction\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Peiqi Yu , cite6†Jinhao Wang , cite7†Xinyi Sui , cite8†Nam Ling , cite9†Wei Wang , cite10†Wei Jiang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Efficient Post-Training Pruning of Large Language Models with Statistical Correction, by Peiqi Yu and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Post-training pruning is an effective approach for reducing the size and inference cost of large language models (LLMs), but existing methods often face a trade-off between pruning quality and computational efficiency. Heuristic pruning methods are efficient but sensitive to activation outliers, while reconstruction-based approaches improve fidelity at the cost of heavy computation.\nL17: In this work, we propose a lightweight post-training pruning framework based on first-order statistical properties of model weights and activations. During pruning, channel-wise statistics are used to calibrate magnitude-based importance scores, reducing bias from activation-dominated channels. After pruning, we apply an analytic energy compensation to correct distributional distortions caused by weight removal. Both steps operate without retraining, gradients, or second-order information.\nL18: Experiments across multiple LLM families, sparsity patterns, and evaluation tasks show that the proposed approach improves pruning performance while maintaining computational cost comparable to heuristic methods. The results suggest that simple statistical corrections can be effective for post-training pruning of LLMs.\nL19: Comments:  | 11 pages, 2 figures, 5 tables\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2602.07375 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2602.07375v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07375†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Wei Jiang [cite16†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Sat, 7 Feb 2026 05:36:17 UTC (296 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Efficient Post-Training Pruning of Large Language Models with Statistical Correction, by Peiqi Yu and 5 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite11†View PDF L36:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite17†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite18†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L45: \nL46: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-02 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite24†cs L51: cite25†cs.LG L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.a