2.07338v1\",\"lineno\":17}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 7 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:Intent Mismatch Causes LLMs to Get Lost in Multi-Turn Conversation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Geng Liu , cite6†Fei Zhu , cite7†Rong Feng , cite8†Changyi Ma , cite9†Shiqi Wang , cite10†Gaofeng Meng L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Intent Mismatch Causes LLMs to Get Lost in Multi-Turn Conversation, by Geng Liu and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Multi-turn conversation has emerged as a predominant interaction paradigm for Large Language Models (LLMs). Users often employ follow-up questions to refine their intent, expecting LLMs to adapt dynamically. However, recent research reveals that LLMs suffer a substantial performance drop in multi-turn settings compared to single-turn interactions with fully specified instructions, a phenomenon termed ``Lost in Conversation'' (LiC).\nL17: While this prior work attributes LiC to model unreliability, we argue that the root cause lies in an intent alignment gap rather than intrinsic capability deficits. In this paper, we first demonstrate that LiC is not a failure of model capability but rather a breakdown in interaction between users and LLMs.\nL18: We theoretically show that scaling model size or improving training alone cannot resolve this gap, as it arises from structural ambiguity in conversational context rather than representational limitations. To address this, we propose to decouple intent understanding from task execution through a Mediator-Assistant architecture.\nL19: By utilizing an experience-driven Mediator to explicate user inputs into explicit, well-structured instructions based on historical interaction patterns, our approach effectively bridges the gap between vague user intent and model interpretation. Experimental results demonstrate that this method significantly mitigates performance degradation in multi-turn conversations across diverse LLMs.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2602.07338 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2602.07338v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07338†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Geng Liu [cite16†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Sat, 7 Feb 2026 03:41:04 UTC (642 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Intent Mismatch Causes LLMs to Get Lost in Multi-Turn Conversation, by Geng Liu and 5 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite11†View PDF L36:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite17†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L45: \nL46: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-02 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite24†cs L51: cite25†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite30†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite31†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite32†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Code, Data, Media\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2602.07120v1] Anchored Decoding: Provably Reducing Copyright Risk for Any Language Model (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07120v1)\nciteturn31571view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07120v1\",\"lineno\":17}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 Feb 2026 (this version), latest version 31 Aug 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:Anchored Decoding: Provably Reducing Copyright Risk for Any Language Model\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Jacqueline He , cite7†Jonathan Hayase , cite8†Wen-tau Yih , cite9†Sewoong Oh , cite10†Luke Zettlemoyer , cite11†Pang Wei Koh L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Anchored Decoding: Provably Reducing Copyright Risk for Any Language Model, by Jacqueline He and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Modern language models (LMs) tend to memorize portions of their training data and emit verbatim spans. When the underlying sources are sensitive or copyright-protected, such reproduction raises issues of consent and compensation for creators and compliance risks for developers.\nL17: We propose Anchored Decoding, a plug-and-play inference-time method for suppressing verbatim copying: it enables decoding from any risky LM trained on mixed-license data by keeping generation in bounded proximity to a permissively trained safe LM. Anchored Decoding adaptively allocates a user-chosen information budget over the generation trajectory and enforces per-step constraints that yield a sequence-level guarantee, enabling a tunable risk-utility trade-off.\nL18: To make Anchored Decoding practically useful, we introduce a new permissively trained safe model (TinyComma 1.8B), as well as Anchored$_{\\mathrm{Byte}}$ Decoding, a byte-level variant of our method that enables cross-vocabulary fusion via the ByteSampler framework (Hayase et al., 2025). We evaluate our methods across six model pairs on long-form evaluations of copyright risk and utility.\nL19: Anchored and Anchored$_{\\mathrm{Byte}}$ Decoding define a new Pareto frontier, preserving near-original fluency and factuality while eliminating up to 75% of the measurable copying gap (averaged over six copying metrics) between the risky baseline and a safe reference, at a modest inference overhead.\nL20: Comments:  | 51 pages, 12 figures, 16 tables. Code is publicly available at cite14†this https URL†github.com L21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2602.07120 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2602.07120v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07120†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jacqueline He [cite18†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Fri, 6 Feb 2026 19:00:14 UTC (1,078 KB)\nL29: cite19†[v2] Mon, 25 May 2026 23:02:20 UTC (1,122 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v3] Mon, 31 Aug 2026 03:52:12 UTC (1,122 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled Anchored Decoding: Provably Reducing Copyright Risk for Any Language Model, by Jacqueline He and 5 other